# The price store: 36 parquet files, 3.7 MB, reproducible from `python -u pipeline/build.py`.
#
# Ignored for now, but no longer because it CANNOT be committed. The old layout was one file per
# symbol (132 files, 14 MB) and every refresh rewrote all of them; since parquet is compressed
# binary that git stores whole, a weekly cron would have added 14 MB to the history every week
# forever. `pipeline/store.py` fixed that: closes are partitioned by calendar year and only
# partitions whose CONTENT changed are written, so a weekly run touches the current year plus the
# event log -- measured at ~216 KB. Committing the store is therefore a 3.7 MB one-off and
# ~11 MB/year after, which is a decision about how much audit trail is worth, not a constraint.
#
# DECIDED 2026-09-04: it stays ignored, and CI refetches. Not because of the size -- because the
# store would be the one committed artifact nothing verifies. `web/public/data/*.json` is checked
# by both test suites on every run, so a wrong number in it fails something; a committed parquet
# would be read by nobody until the next build, and a corrupted partition would sit in the history
# looking authoritative. The event log makes the store auditable where it matters (what changed,
# when, and how the vendor's series moved), and that is what `--skip-fetch` and a local store are
# for. Revisit only if a run ever needs to be reproduced against prices the vendor has since
# restated -- that is the one thing refetching genuinely cannot do.
#
# web/public/data/*.json is the opposite case and IS committed on purpose: it is 432 KB of text,
# it is what the SPA loads, and GitHub Pages has no other way to serve it. That asymmetry is the
# whole hosting design -- the repo is the database.
pipeline/data/prices/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.venv/

node_modules/
web/dist/
*.tsbuildinfo

# The mutation harness's staged copy of `web/`. Recreated per run and removed on exit, but
# ignored anyway: if the run is interrupted it survives, and a stray tree of half-mutated
# sources is the one thing that must never look like a change somebody meant.
web/.mutate/

.DS_Store
